NAME
       basename - print a path without its directories (and suffix)

SYNOPSIS
       basename PATH [SUFFIX]
       basename -a [-s SUFFIX] PATH...

DESCRIPTION
       basename removes everything up to the last slash from PATH and prints
       what is left: the name of the file itself. If SUFFIX is given and the
       name ends with it, the suffix is removed too. It only works on the
       text; the file does not need to exist.

       In scripts it is often used as $(basename "$f") inside a loop.

OPTIONS
       -a, --multiple
              Treat every argument as a PATH.

       -s SUFFIX, --suffix=SUFFIX
              Remove this suffix (implies -a).

EXAMPLES
           basename /etc/sysconfig/network     prints network
           basename /root/report.csv .csv      prints report

       Copy every .conf file to a .bak copy with a new name:

           for f in /etc/*.conf; do cp "$f" /tmp/$(basename "$f" .conf).bak; done

SEE ALSO
       dirname(1), realpath(1)
